## Décisions de ce plan (complètent D-K et D-L)

- **D-N** `cut-upstream` : préflight `OpCut` (dans la commande), `CutUpstream` = lecture, `scale
  --replicas=0` si l'amont n'est pas déjà coupé, puis attente qu'**aucun** pod de l'amont n'existe
  (un pod en `Terminating` compte). Idempotent. Ne touche ni Nexus ni le descripteur.
- **D-O** `restore-upstream` : préflight `OpRestore`, `RestoreUpstream` = identifiants stockés (avant
  toute écriture) → `EnsureUpstream` (1 réplica, attente du prêt) → `UnblockProxy` (`PUT` de la
  configuration du proxy de `repositorySpecs(p)`, puis `POST .../docker-proxy/invalidate-cache`) →
  vérification par port-forward que chaque image amont a le digest du jeu de données (CA4 « contenu
  d'avant la coupure ») → relecture par `docker-proxy` des seules images `PullThrough` avec
  `bench-pull`, digest égal (rafraîchit le cache invalidé : parade à H-STALE pour la coupure
  suivante). N'écrit pas le descripteur.
- **D-P** `reset` : `Reset` = préflight `OpReset` → refus d'un amont personnalisé → identifiants
  stockés **et vérifiés par un `GET` admin** (un mot de passe refusé arrête tout avant la première
  suppression) → `DELETE` `docker-group`, `docker-proxy`, `docker-hosted` (404 = déjà fait) →
  recyclage du stockage de l'amont (`CutUpstream`, label vérifié, `delete pvc --wait=false`,
  attente `NotFound` bornée par `DeleteTimeout`, `apply` du seul PVC, `EnsureUpstream`) →
  `Provision` avec `storedPasswordOnly` → attente bornée (30 s) que `docker-hosted` et
  `docker-proxy` ne listent plus rien (H-REPODEL) → `Seed`. La commande lit le descripteur
  précédent avant tout (comme `seed`) et le réécrit après succès (attendu : identique octet pour
  octet).
- **D-Q** `up` : si l'amont lu après `Deploy` est `Cut`, `up` appelle `UnblockProxy` après
  `Provision` (`Hypothèse : up ramène le banc à son état nominal, cache négatif compris`).
- Aucun nouveau `ErrKind` : REST → `ProvisionFailed`/`RESTUnreachable` (`asBenchError`), jeu de
  données → `SeedFailed`, attentes → `Deadline`, kubectl → `classifyKubectl`.

### Étape 2 — `CutUpstream` (parallélisable avec 1, 3)

- `upstream.go` :
  ```go
  // CutUpstream scales the bench upstream to zero replicas and returns once no
  // pod of it is left, a terminating one included. It runs no preflight.
  func CutUpstream(ctx context.Context, r Runner, clk Clock, p Params, out io.Writer) error
  func (d *deployer) waitUpstreamGone(ctx context.Context) error
  ```
  Ordre : `out` nil accepté ; amont personnalisé → `customUpstreamError` sans appel ; `ctx.Err()` ;
  `readUpstream` : `Missing` → même erreur que `EnsureUpstream` (à factoriser en
  `missingUpstreamError(d)`) ; `Cut` → aucune écriture ; sinon `scaleUpstream(ctx, 0)` une fois.
  Puis `waitUpstreamGone` : `d.read(podsArgs)` toutes les `upstreamPollInterval`, fin quand la
  liste est vide ; échéance `p.Timeout` → `Deadline` qui cite le ou les pods restants et
  `NEXUS_BENCH_TIMEOUT`. Lignes `upstream: ...`.
- Tests (`upstream_test.go`, `upstreamKubectl` : ajouter des rounds après le scale qui portent un
  pod `Terminating` puis aucun) :
  - prêt → une seule écriture `scale deployment/bench-upstream --replicas=0 -n <ns>`, attente
    jusqu'au round vide, sommeils de 2 s ;
  - déjà coupé, sans pod → aucune écriture, aucune attente ; déjà coupé avec un pod qui termine →
    aucune écriture, attente ;
  - `Starting` → scale 0 (on coupe aussi un amont qui démarre) ;
  - absent → erreur citant `mise run nexus-bench:up`, aucune écriture ;
  - pod qui ne part jamais → `Deadline` qui nomme le pod, dans un intervalle de l'échéance ;
  - échec du scale → classé, pas de nouvelle tentative ; lecture en échec → `*BenchError` ;
  - contexte annulé → rendu tel quel ; amont personnalisé → refus, zéro appel ;
  - journal des écritures : uniquement `scale` sur `bench-upstream`.
- **Fin** : `go test -count=1 ./internal/nexusbench -run 'Upstream'` vert.

### Étape 3 — REST : suppression, déblocage, configuration du proxy (parallélisable avec 1, 2)

- `provision.go` : `func repositorySpecs(p Params) []repositorySpec` (corps actuel de
  `repositories()`, qui ne lit que `pv.p`) ; `pv.repositories()` la délègue (refactor sans
  changement : tests de provision inchangés et verts).
- `repoapi.go` :
  ```go
  // benchRepositories are the only repositories reset may delete, in the order it
  // deletes them: the group first, as it names the two others.
  var benchRepositories = []string{dockerGroupRepository, dockerProxyRepository, dockerHostedRepository}

  func (c *client) deleteRepository(ctx context.Context, name string) (existed bool, err error) // DELETE /v1/repositories/{name}; 404 -> false, nil; name outside benchRepositories -> error, no request
  func (c *client) invalidateCache(ctx context.Context, name string) error                     // POST /v1/repositories/{name}/invalidate-cache
  func (c *client) unblockProxy(ctx context.Context, p Params) error                          // GET proxy (404 -> ProvisionFailed "run up"), PUT repositorySpecs(p)[proxy].config, invalidateCache
  func (c *client) requireAdmin(ctx context.Context) error                                    // one GET as admin; 401/403 -> ProvisionFailed "nothing was changed"

  // UnblockProxy puts docker-proxy back in service after its upstream came back:
  // the configuration Provision writes, sent again, then its cache invalidated.
  func UnblockProxy(ctx context.Context, store SecretStore, baseURL string, p Params, out io.Writer) error
  ```
  `UnblockProxy` refuse un amont personnalisé sans requête ; erreurs par `asBenchError` ; lignes
  `restore: ...`. Codes REST selon l'étape 0 (204 attendu).
- `fakenexus_test.go` : routes `DELETE {pathRepositories}/{name}` (204, 404 inconnu ; supprime
  aussi les composants du dépôt, sauf `KeepComponentsAfterDelete()` qui simule H-REPODEL fausse
  pendant N lectures) et `POST {pathRepositories}/{name}/invalidate-cache` (204 pour proxy et
  groupe, 400 pour un hosted) ; état `blocked` du proxy (`AutoBlockProxy()`) levé par un `PUT`
  du proxy ; `NegativeCached(name)` vidé par `invalidate-cache` ; en-tête de doc des routes complété.
- Tests (`repoapi_test.go`) : `deleteRepository` 204 → `true`, 404 → `false`, 403 →
  `ProvisionFailed` sans mot de passe dans le texte, `maven-releases` → erreur **sans requête**
  (compteur du fake) ; `unblockProxy` : séquence exacte `GET`, `PUT .../docker/proxy/docker-proxy`
  avec le corps de `repositorySpecs(p)` (dont `pathEnabled: true`, `autoBlock: true`), `POST
  .../docker-proxy/invalidate-cache` ; proxy absent → erreur qui cite `up`, ni `PUT` ni `POST` ;
  `requireAdmin` avec un mot de passe refusé → `ProvisionFailed` ; `UnblockProxy` sans secrets →
  « run up » ; amont personnalisé → zéro requête ; `requireQuiet` partout.
- **Fin** : `go test -count=1 ./internal/nexusbench -run 'Provision|Repo|Unblock|Fake'` vert.

### Étape 4 — `RestoreUpstream` (dépend de 3 ; attend le verdict H-UNBLOCK de l'étape 0)
package nexusbench

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"slices"
	"time"
)

// upstreamPollInterval separates two rounds of the wait for the upstream. It is
// shorter than pollInterval: a registry is ready within seconds of its image
// being local, and the wait has no host to probe.
const upstreamPollInterval = 2 * time.Second

// UpstreamState is what the cluster says about the bench upstream registry.
type UpstreamState int

// The states of the upstream, from the least to the most alive.
const (
	// UpstreamMissing: the Deployment bench-upstream does not exist.
	UpstreamMissing UpstreamState = iota
	// UpstreamCut: the Deployment exists with zero replicas.
	UpstreamCut
	// UpstreamStarting: one replica is wanted and no pod of it is ready yet.
	UpstreamStarting
	// UpstreamReady: a pod of the upstream that is not terminating is ready.
	UpstreamReady
)

// ReadUpstream reads the state of the bench upstream with read-only kubectl
// calls: it writes nothing and waits for nothing. A pod that fails to pull is
// Starting, not an error: diagnosing is the job of the wait. It runs no
// preflight; the caller does. Every failure is a *BenchError, but a cancelled
// context, which is returned as is.
func ReadUpstream(ctx context.Context, r Runner, p Params) (UpstreamState, error) {
	d := &deployer{pf: preflight{r: r, p: p}, w: upstreamWorkload(), tag: "upstream", out: io.Discard}
	return d.readUpstream(ctx)
}

// EnsureUpstream brings the bench upstream to one ready replica, and returns
// when it is ready or fails with a diagnosis. It reads first and writes only
// when the upstream is cut: one scale to one replica, then the wait. A starting
// upstream is waited for without a write; a ready one is left alone. The
// Deployment itself is applied by Deploy, so a missing one is an error. A
// custom upstream (Params.UpstreamIsBench is false) is refused before any call:
// the bench scales what it deploys and nothing else. The wait polls the pods
// and the claim of the upstream only, until Params.Timeout. It runs no
// preflight; the caller does. Every failure is a *BenchError, but a cancelled
// context, which is returned as is. A nil out discards the progress lines.
func EnsureUpstream(ctx context.Context, r Runner, clk Clock, p Params, out io.Writer) error {
	if out == nil {
		out = io.Discard
	}
	if !p.UpstreamIsBench() {
		return customUpstreamError(p)
	}
	// The runner of a test may ignore ctx; the first call must not depend on it.
	if err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {
		return err
	}
	d := &deployer{pf: preflight{r: r, p: p}, w: upstreamWorkload(), tag: "upstream", clk: clk, out: out}
	state, err := d.readUpstream(ctx)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	switch state {
	case UpstreamMissing:
		return &BenchError{
			Kind:  Unclassified,
			Cause: fmt.Sprintf("the Deployment %s does not exist in namespace %q", upstreamDeployment, p.Namespace),
			Check: "run `mise run nexus-bench:up`, which applies it, or " + d.pf.command("get", "deployment", upstreamDeployment, "-n", p.Namespace),
		}
	case UpstreamReady:
		d.say("the upstream is ready")
		return nil
	case UpstreamCut:
		d.say("scaling the upstream to one replica")
		if err := d.scaleUpstream(ctx, 1); err != nil {
			return err
		}
	case UpstreamStarting:
		d.say("the upstream is starting")
	}
	return d.waitUpstream(ctx)
}

// customUpstreamError refuses an upstream the bench does not deploy.
func customUpstreamError(p Params) *BenchError {
	return &BenchError{
		Kind: Unclassified,
		Cause: fmt.Sprintf("%s points at an upstream the bench did not deploy in namespace %q, and the bench scales only its own upstream",
			envUpstreamURL, p.Namespace),
		Check: "unset " + envUpstreamURL + " to use the bench upstream, or start and stop your own upstream registry yourself",
	}
}

// readUpstream is ReadUpstream on a deployer.
func (d *deployer) readUpstream(ctx context.Context) (UpstreamState, error) {
	var dep struct {
		Spec struct {
			Replicas *int `json:"replicas"`
		} `json:"spec"`
	}
	args := []string{"get", "deployment", upstreamDeployment, "-n", d.pf.p.Namespace, "-o", "json"}
	found, err := d.fetch(ctx, "read the Deployment "+upstreamDeployment, args, &dep, true)
	if err != nil {
		return UpstreamMissing, err
	}
	if !found {
		return UpstreamMissing, nil
	}
	// The manifest leaves the count out, so the API server defaults it to one.
	if dep.Spec.Replicas != nil && *dep.Spec.Replicas == 0 {
		return UpstreamCut, nil
	}
	var pods struct {
		Items []podObject `json:"items"`
	}
	if err := d.read(ctx, "read the "+d.w.name+" pod", d.podsArgs(), &pods); err != nil {
		return UpstreamMissing, err
	}
	if slices.ContainsFunc(pods.Items, podObject.ready) {
		return UpstreamReady, nil
	}
	return UpstreamStarting, nil
}

// scaleUpstream is the one write of EnsureUpstream. A failed write is not
// retried: the scale is not what a second try would fix.
func (d *deployer) scaleUpstream(ctx context.Context, replicas int) error {
	args := []string{"scale", "deployment/" + upstreamDeployment, fmt.Sprintf("--replicas=%d", replicas), "-n", d.pf.p.Namespace, applyTimeout}
	_, errOut, err := d.pf.r.Run(ctx, nil, args...)
	if err == nil {
		return nil
	}
	if cerr := ctx.Err(); cerr != nil {
		return cerr
	}
	return d.pf.failure("scale the Deployment "+upstreamDeployment, args, string(errOut), err)
}

// waitUpstream polls until a pod of the upstream is ready, a diagnosis fails the
// run, or the deadline passes. Like the wait of Deploy, it looks at the deadline
// after a round, so the cluster is read once more at the very end.
func (d *deployer) waitUpstream(ctx context.Context) error {
	deadline := d.clk.Now().Add(d.pf.p.Timeout)
	for {
		obs, err := d.round(ctx)
		if err != nil {
			return err
		}
		if err := ctx.Err(); err != nil {
			return err
		}
		if slices.ContainsFunc(obs.pods, podObject.ready) {
			d.say("the upstream is ready")
			return nil
		}
		if !d.clk.Now().Before(deadline) {
			return d.upstreamDeadline(obs)
		}
		if err := d.clk.Sleep(ctx, upstreamPollInterval); err != nil {
			return err
		}
	}
}

// upstreamDeadline is the end of a wait that ran out of time. A pending reading
// that was still there says more than the deadline does.
func (d *deployer) upstreamDeadline(obs observation) error {
	if d.candidate != nil {
		return d.candidate
	}
	p := d.pf.p
	return &BenchError{
		Kind:  Deadline,
		Cause: fmt.Sprintf("the upstream %s was not ready within %s; last state: %s", upstreamDeployment, p.Timeout, obs.describe()),
		Check: describePodCommand(p, d.w) + ", then raise " + envTimeout + " if the image pull or the start is only slow",
	}
}

// ready reports whether the pod is not terminating and its Ready condition is
// True.
func (pod podObject) ready() bool {
	if pod.Metadata.DeletionTimestamp != nil {
		return false
	}
	for _, c := range pod.Status.Conditions {
		if c.Type == "Ready" {
			return c.Status == "True"
		}
	}
	return false
}
package nexusbench

import (
	"bytes"
	"context"
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"net/http"
	"net/url"
	"slices"
	"strings"
	"time"
)

const (
	// clientTimeout bounds every REST call, connection included.
	clientTimeout = 15 * time.Second
	// maxResponseBytes is the most the client reads from one answer. A larger
	// one is an error, and its content is never decoded.
	maxResponseBytes = 1 << 20
	// drainBytes is how much of an unwanted answer is read so the connection
	// can be reused.
	drainBytes = 4 << 10
)

// APIError is a REST call the instance answered with a status outside 2xx. It
// carries the operation and the status only: no URL, no request body, no answer
// body, because any of them could hold a credential.
type APIError struct {
	// Op says what the call was for, e.g. "create user bench-pull".
	Op     string
	Status int
	// RemoteURLBlocked is true for a 400 whose validation errors name
	// proxy.remoteUrl: Nexus 3.98.1 resolves the host of a proxy's remote URL
	// when it saves the repository and refuses one that does not resolve. Only
	// this verdict is kept, never the text of the answer.
	RemoteURLBlocked bool
	// RemoteURLPrivate is true for a 400 whose validation errors name
	// proxy.remoteUrl and say its host resolves to a private address: the SSRF
	// protection of Nexus 3.98 refuses it unless the host is allowed (H-SSRF).
	// Like RemoteURLBlocked, only the verdict is kept.
	RemoteURLPrivate bool
}

// Error returns "<Op>: HTTP <status> <text>".
func (e *APIError) Error() string {
	return strings.TrimSpace(fmt.Sprintf("%s: HTTP %d %s", e.Op, e.Status, http.StatusText(e.Status)))
}

// transportError is a call that got no answer: connection refused, reset,
// timeout. The cause stays in Err; its text carries the URL and is not printed.
type transportError struct {
	Op  string
	Err error
}

func (e *transportError) Error() string { return "no answer to " + e.Op }
func (e *transportError) Unwrap() error { return e.Err }

// tooLargeError is an answer past maxResponseBytes.
type tooLargeError struct{ Op string }

func (e *tooLargeError) Error() string {
	return fmt.Sprintf("the answer to %s is larger than %d bytes", e.Op, maxResponseBytes)
}

// tooManyError is a listing past maxListedComponents.
type tooManyError struct{ Op string }

func (e *tooManyError) Error() string {
	return fmt.Sprintf("the answer to %s lists more than %d components", e.Op, maxListedComponents)
}

// decodeError is an answer that is not the JSON the client expects.
type decodeError struct {
	Op  string
	Err error
}

func (e *decodeError) Error() string { return "unexpected answer to " + e.Op }
func (e *decodeError) Unwrap() error { return e.Err }

// client is the REST client of one Nexus instance. It follows no redirection
// (an answer that moves the call elsewhere would carry the credentials with
// it), reads at most maxResponseBytes of an answer, and never logs a header.
type client struct {
	base     string
	user     string
	REDACTED string
	http     *http.Client
}

// newClient returns a client for baseURL, authenticating with Basic auth as user
// unless user is empty (the calls that need no account).
func newClient(baseURL, user, REDACTED string) *client {
	transport := http.DefaultTransport.(*http.Transport).Clone()
	// The bench is reached directly: a proxy named by the environment would
	// receive the credentials.
	transport.Proxy = nil
	return &client{
		base:     strings.TrimRight(baseURL, "/"),
		user:     user,
		REDACTED: REDACTED,
		http: &http.Client{
			Timeout:       clientTimeout,
			Transport:     transport,
			CheckRedirect: func(*http.Request, []*http.Request) error { return http.ErrUseLastResponse },
		},
	}
}

// closeIdle drops the connections the client keeps open.
func (c *client) closeIdle() { c.http.CloseIdleConnections() }

// request is one REST call.
type request struct {
	op          string
	method      string
	path        string
	query       url.Values
	contentType string
	body        []byte
}

// response is what the instance answered.
type response struct {
	status int
	header http.Header
	body   []byte
	// remoteURLBlocked is the verdict of remoteURLRefused on a 400; the body of
	// an answer outside 2xx is read for it and dropped.
	remoteURLBlocked bool
	// remoteURLPrivate is the verdict of remoteURLPrivate on the same 400.
	remoteURLPrivate bool
}

// do sends r and returns the answer whatever its status. The error is a
// *transportError or a *tooLargeError.
func (c *client) do(ctx context.Context, r request) (response, error) {
	target := c.base + r.path
	if len(r.query) > 0 {
		target += "?" + r.query.Encode()
	}
	var body io.Reader
	if r.body != nil {
		body = bytes.NewReader(r.body)
	}
	req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, r.method, target, body)
	if err != nil {
		return response{}, &transportError{Op: r.op, Err: err}
	}
	req.Header.Set("Accept", "application/json")
	if r.contentType != "" {
		req.Header.Set("Content-Type", r.contentType)
	}
	if c.user != "" {
		req.SetBasicAuth(c.user, c.REDACTED)
	}
	resp, err := c.http.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		return response{}, &transportError{Op: r.op, Err: err}
	}
	defer func() { _ = resp.Body.Close() }()
	if resp.StatusCode < 200 || resp.StatusCode > 299 {
		refused, _ := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(resp.Body, drainBytes))
		badRequest := resp.StatusCode == http.StatusBadRequest
		return response{
			status: resp.StatusCode, header: resp.Header,
			remoteURLBlocked: badRequest && remoteURLRefused(refused),
			remoteURLPrivate: badRequest && remoteURLPrivate(refused),
		}, nil
	}
	data, err := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(resp.Body, maxResponseBytes+1))
	if err != nil {
		return response{}, &transportError{Op: r.op, Err: err}
	}
	if len(data) > maxResponseBytes {
		return response{}, &tooLargeError{Op: r.op}
	}
	return response{status: resp.StatusCode, header: resp.Header, body: data}, nil
}

// expect sends r and turns a status outside 2xx into an *APIError.
func (c *client) expect(ctx context.Context, r request) (response, error) {
	resp, err := c.do(ctx, r)
	if err != nil {
		return resp, err
	}
	if resp.status < 200 || resp.status > 299 {
		return resp, &APIError{Op: r.op, Status: resp.status, RemoteURLBlocked: resp.remoteURLBlocked, RemoteURLPrivate: resp.remoteURLPrivate}
	}
	return resp, nil
}

// remoteURLRefused reports whether body is a Nexus validation error list with
// an entry on proxy.remoteUrl whose message says the host did not resolve:
// [{"id":"HelperBean.proxy.remoteUrl","message":"... Failed to resolve host: ..."}].
// Only that verdict leaves the function: no text of the answer is kept. The
// content type of the answer is vendor specific, so only the JSON is read; a
// body past drainBytes was cut and is not trusted.
func remoteURLRefused(body []byte) bool {
	return remoteURLMessageHas(body, "resolve host")
}

// remoteURLPrivate reports whether body is a validation error list with an entry
// on proxy.remoteUrl that says the host resolves to a private address, which is
// what the SSRF protection of Nexus 3.98 answers (observed on 3.98.1-01:
// "Proxy URL blocked: Host resolves to private/local IP address(es): ..."). Same
// rules as remoteURLRefused: only the verdict leaves the function.
func remoteURLPrivate(body []byte) bool {
	return remoteURLMessageHas(body, "resolves to private")
}

// remoteURLMessageHas reports whether body is a validation error list with an
// entry on proxy.remoteUrl whose message contains fragment, case-insensitive.
func remoteURLMessageHas(body []byte, fragment string) bool {
	if len(body) > drainBytes {
		return false
	}
	var errs []validationError
	if err := json.Unmarshal(body, &errs); err != nil {
		return false
	}
	return slices.ContainsFunc(errs, func(e validationError) bool {
		return strings.HasSuffix(e.ID, "proxy.remoteUrl") &&
			strings.Contains(strings.ToLower(e.Message), fragment)
	})
}

// validationError is one entry of a Nexus validation error list. The message is
// only matched by remoteURLRefused, never stored or shown.
type validationError struct {
	ID      string `json:"id"`
	Message string `json:"message"`
}

// get reads path and decodes the JSON answer into out (nil: not decoded). A 404
// is found == false without an error.
func (c *client) get(ctx context.Context, op, path string, query url.Values, out any) (found bool, err error) {
	resp, err := c.do(ctx, request{op: op, method: http.MethodGet, path: path, query: query})
	if err != nil {
		return false, err
	}
	switch {
	case resp.status == http.StatusNotFound:
		return false, nil
	case resp.status < 200 || resp.status > 299:
		return false, &APIError{Op: op, Status: resp.status}
	}
	if out == nil {
		return true, nil
	}
	if err := json.Unmarshal(resp.body, out); err != nil {
		return false, &decodeError{Op: op, Err: err}
	}
	return true, nil
}

// read is get for an object that has to exist: a 404 is an *APIError.
func (c *client) read(ctx context.Context, op, path string, query url.Values, out any) error {
	found, err := c.get(ctx, op, path, query, out)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	if !found {
		return &APIError{Op: op, Status: http.StatusNotFound}
	}
	return nil
}

// write sends payload as JSON with method and expects a 2xx.
func (c *client) write(ctx context.Context, op, method, path string, payload any) error {
	data, err := json.Marshal(payload)
	if err != nil {
		return &decodeError{Op: op, Err: err}
	}
	_, err = c.expect(ctx, request{op: op, method: method, path: path, contentType: "application/json", body: data})
	return err
}

